From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: caskade <caskade@localhost>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 11:37:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Place the IME at the caret, tell it the field's purpose,
 commit on blur

Blitz enabled the IME with no capabilities, so winit dropped every caret
area it was sent (it only accepts them once the cursor-area capability is
asked for), and the area it computed was the input's whole content box,
offset twice by its position. An IME's candidate window opened wherever
the platform put it, often away from the text.

- `ShellProvider::enable_ime` takes the field's purpose (from its `type`:
  password, email, number, tel, url) and a first caret area; blitz-shell
  asks winit for the cursor-area and hint-and-purpose capabilities, so a
  password field is hidden and sensitive to the IME.
- Each resolve sends the focused field's caret area when it moved
  (parley's `ime_cursor_area`: the preedit, or the line up to the caret),
  in the window's logical pixels, after the field's and the page's
  scrolling and the zoom.
- A field losing the focus mid-composition commits it, as browsers do,
  with its `input` and `compositionend`, instead of keeping stale preedit.

Tests in `text_input_ime.rs` drive a composition through the event driver.
